# --- s049 Book I d.15 q.3
n("n785","s049-01","vtrum relatio quecunque possit fundare respectum","la","source","A question whether a relation can be the foundation of a further relation (relation of relation). Its place in Scotus's dd. 14–16 has not been identified; it may come from a later Scotist (compare the *Reportatio* or Francis of Meyronnes).","uncertain")
n("n786","s049-01","necessitas constituit filium secundum Hylarium","la","source","Attributed to Hilary of Poitiers. Not located; compare *De Trin.* (Hilary) III–VII on the Son's birth by nature, not will.","to-verify")
n("n787","s049-02","si relatio potest immediate fundare substantiam","la","textual","Thus A (*Primum si relatio …*). B reads *Primum relatio potest immediate fundare substantiam* (without *si*). Both are elliptical for *Primum vtrum*.","verified")
n("n788","s049-03","dicit sanctus doctor","la","historical","*Sanctus doctor*: Thomas Aquinas (canonized 1323), set against *doctor* without epithet, i.e. Scotus, the *Doctor subtilis*.","certain")
n("n789","s049-03","prima parte. q. 39. articulo. 2°.","la","source","As printed. *ST* I q. 39 a. 2 concerns the plurality of persons and essence, so the reference may be corrupt. Aquinas's teaching that the relation does not found the substance is better sought in *ST* I q. 28 aa. 1–2.","uncertain")
n("n790","s049-03","causaliter","la","textual","Thus A and B. The argument requires *causalitas* (compare *ista causalitas* immediately after); the translation reads ‘causality’.","verified")
n("n791","s049-03","philosophi primo phisicorum","la","source","Aristotle, *Physics* I (cf. 9, 192a13–14, matter with form as *concausa*). The wording looks like an *auctoritas* rather than a quotation: compare the *Auctoritates Aristotelis*, which has a similar maxim that matter and form are causes of the accidents.","to-verify")
n("n792","s049-05","fundat respectum primum. quia respectum quem fundat","la","textual","A is corrupt here. B reads *fundat respectum quadruplicem. Primus respectus quem fundat est relatio distinctionis*, which the enumeration that follows (*Secundus … Tertius … Quartus*) requires. The translation follows B.","verified")
n("n793","s049-05","possit fundare respectum. dicitur","la","textual","B reads *fundari* for A's *fundare* (passive spiration ‘be founded’ vs ‘found’). Not substantive for the solution, which concerns relations founded *by* a divine relation.","verified")
n("n794","s049-05","secundum Dyonisium","la","source","Pseudo-Dionysius on order among the divine properties; perhaps *De div. nom.* II. Not located.","to-verify")
n("n795","s049-06","⟨.h.⟩","la","textual","A prints a single letter between points, *b* or *h*. B reads *minor* here (*pater est relatio minor*?). Neither yields a clear sense; the word is left untranslated.","uncertain")
n("n796","s049-06","⟨diffinibilis⟩","la","textual","A is barely legible in the reproduction and could be read *dissimilis*. B clearly has *diffinibilis*, which the answer (*diffinitionem minime*) requires.","verified")
n("n797","s049-03","causality","en","translation","A and B *causaliter*; read *causalitas*.")
n("n798","s049-05","[fourfold]","en","translation","Supplied from B (*quadruplicem*); A has *primum. quia*.")
n("n799","s049-02","immediately found a substance","en","translation","*fundare substantiam*: literally ‘found a substance’, but the argument shows that the sense is ‘be founded immediately on a substance’ (*fundatur super materiam et formam*). The same applies in 049-03.")

# --- B collation (full): B67L–B68L
upd("n789", append=" B also *q. 39. arti. 2.*")
